 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer: no, not at the moment.** Inflation is above the RBA's 2–3% target and has been rising over the past year.\n\n**Latest figures (August 2026, annual change)**\n- **Headline CPI:** 4.0%. It was 1.9% in June 2025 and peaked at 4.6% in March 2026. It eased to 3.5% in July, then rose again.\n- **Trimmed mean (the RBA's preferred underlying measure):** 3.6%. It has risen steadily from 2.8% in June 2025, so the pressure is broad and not confined to a few volatile items.\n- **Rents:** 3.6%, down from 4.2% in June 2025 and October 2025. This is easing slowly, but it is still high.\n- **Electricity:** 13.2%. It was 37% in October 2025 and again in February 2026. It is a big contributor to headline inflation, though the rate of increase has slowed.\n\n**How the RBA has responded**\n- The cash rate was cut to about 3.6% in late 2025.\n- It has since been raised to about 4.35%, with the monthly average at 4.36% in September 2026. The increases ran from February to May 2026.\n- Higher rates are now in place, and underlying inflation hasn't turned down yet.\n\n**Labour market**\n- The unemployment rate has risen from 4.1% in December 2025 to 4.7% in September 2026. That suggests demand is cooling, which should eventually reduce price pressure.\n- It also makes the RBA's choice harder, because it has to weigh inflation against rising unemployment.\n\n**Assessment**\n- Underlying inflation is about 0.6–1 percentage point above the top of the target band.\n- Its upward trend has stopped accelerating: trimmed mean has been flat at 3.6% for three months.\n- It is not yet clearly falling, and the August headline rate rose again.\n- Inflation would count as under control once trimmed mean is clearly heading back toward 3% or lower. The rate rises and a softer jobs market make that possible, but it hasn't happened yet.",
